		{
			"id": "111211619712325346",
			"parentId": "111211560304497702",
			"url": "https://piaille.fr/@tbroyer/111211619574872444",
			"author": {
				"name": "Thomas Broyer",
				"url": "https://piaille.fr/@tbroyer",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/342/716/921/240/219/original/2039b34c330e245c.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-10-10T16:17:24.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://social.bennypowers.dev/@bp\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>bp</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> That doesn't mean DSD is what you need every time: e.g. GitHub's relative-time element, or an element you use to wrap a table that turns it into a sortable/filterable table.<br>DSD won't help here as what you want is only progressively enhancing the light DOM (the elements could use a shadow tree, but a declarative one would buy you nothing)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "111211647860128711",
			"parentId": "111211619712325346",
